{ Welcome to intermediate tactics. You are White. First trick: deflection. Pull a defender off its post, and whatever it was guarding falls. }
18... Nd7 { His knight drops back to d7, and that is a mistake: his rook had to leave the corner. Now only his queen guards the rook on a8. Take the knight with your rook and make her choose. [%cal Yd2d7,Rc8a8] } 19. Rxd7 { The rook grabs the knight. If his queen takes back, she leaves c8, and the rook on a8 has no guard. [%cal Rc8a8] } (19. Bd4 { A nice square, but it does nothing to his queen, and she keeps guarding the rook on a8. } 19... Bf6 20. h3 h6 21. a4 Bxd4 22. Rxd4 { The bishops come off, his queen never left c8, and the game is level. The deflection chance is gone. [%csl Ga8] }) (19. Bd6 { Trading bishops never touches his queen, so she keeps guarding the rook on a8. } 19... Bxd6 20. Rxd6 Rb8 21. Qe4 Qc7 { Level: his rook left the corner for b8, nothing of his can be won, and his queen now hits your rook on d6. [%csl Gc7] }) 19... Qxd7 { His queen takes back. She has left c8, and nothing guards the rook on a8 now. Take it with check. [%csl Ra8][%cal Yf3a8] } 20. Qxa8+ { Check, and the rook is yours. Your rook went for his knight, and then this rook came for free: you are a knight up. That is deflection. } (20. Bxg7 { The pawn on g7 is small change, and it gives his rook time to leave the corner. } 20... Rd8 21. Bh6 Qd4 22. g3 Qf6 { His rook escaped to d8. You gave a rook for a knight, one pawn does not pay for it, and he is better. [%csl Rd8] }) (20. h4 { A quiet pawn move lets his rook walk out of the corner. } 20... Rd8 21. a3 f6 22. Bf4 b5 { The rook got out to d8. You gave a rook for a knight and never got more, and he is winning. [%csl Rd8] }) 1-0


