{ You are Black. Your queen has reached e2, deep in his camp, and your bishop on c5 aims at his king side. Deflection: find it. }
24. Rae1 { His rook comes to e1 to attack your queen, and that is the mistake: his other rook now has two jobs. Find the deflection. } 24... Bxf2+ { The bishop gives itself up on f2 with check. King moves lose even more, so his rook on f1 has to take it. } (24... Qh5 { The queen retreats, and his rooks keep both e1 and f2 covered. } 25. Re5 Bd4 26. Rf5 Qh3 { His rooks are active, he is a pawn up, and he is better. [%csl Rf5] }) (24... Qf3 { The queen stays active, but his bishop comes to e3 and blocks yours. } 25. Be3 Bxe3 26. Rxe3 Qb7 { Your bishop is gone, his rook on e3 guards everything, and he is better. [%csl Re3] }) (24... Qg4 { This offers the queens off, and without her your attack on e1 is gone. } 25. Qxg4 Nxg4 26. Bf5 Nxf2 { Your knight took a pawn, but his bishop on f5 now attacks your rook on c8, and he is winning. [%cal Rf5c8] }) (24... Qxe1 { You take the rook that attacked you, and his other rook takes your queen. [%cal Rf1e1] } 25. Rxe1 { A queen for a rook. He is winning. [%csl Re1] }) (24... Qxf1+ { A check, but it just hands him your queen for a rook. [%cal Rg1f1] } 25. Kxf1 { A queen for a rook, and your attack is over. He is winning. [%csl Rf1] }) (24... Qxc2 { You grab his bishop, and his queen takes yours. [%cal Ra4c2] } 25. Qxc2 { A queen for a bishop. He is winning. [%csl Rc2] }) 25. Rxf2 { His rook takes on f2. Look at what it used to guard. } 25... Qxe1+ { Check, and the rook on e1 is yours. The other rook left f1 to take your bishop, so nothing guarded it any more: a rook for a bishop. That is deflection. } (25... Qxf2+ { This takes the wrong rook: his king stands right beside f2. } 26. Kxf2 h6 27. Bf5 Rf8 { You gave your queen and bishop for one rook, and you are lost. [%csl Rf2] }) (25... Qa6 { The queen runs, but all your bishop bought was a pawn. } 26. Bxf6 Qxa4 27. Bxa4 gxf6 { The queens are off and you are a whole piece down. He is winning. [%csl Rf6] }) (25... Rxc2 { You take his bishop with the rook, but his rook on f2 takes your queen. [%cal Rf2e2] } 26. Rfxe2 { Your queen went for a bishop, and the most your rook on c2 can do now is trade itself for his. He is winning. [%csl Re2] }) (25... Qxc2 { You take the bishop, and his rook takes your queen. [%cal Rf2c2] } 26. Rxc2 { His rook took your queen, and a bishop is all you got for her. He is winning. [%csl Rc2] }) 0-1


