{ You are White, a piece down for a pawn, but his king is still stuck in the middle. The obvious move will not be the best one. }
21... Qb7 { He offers the queens off on b7, and that is the mistake: his knight should have jumped to the middle instead. Look before you take. } 22. Rd8+ { Check! Your bishop guards d8, so only his rook can take, and that rook was the only guard of his queen on b7. [%cal Gb6d8] } (22. Qxb7 { Taking the queen at once lets his rook take back: a queen for a queen, and you have won nothing. } 22... Rxb7 23. Bd4 Be7 24. a4 O-O { The queens are gone, and you stay a piece down for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rb7] }) (22. Bd4 { The bishop centralises, but his queen takes yours on f3 first. } 22... Qxf3 23. gxf3 Nd5 24. Rfe1 Kd7 { The queens are off, and you are still a piece down for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rd5] }) (22. Qxf6 { You take the knight, and his pawn takes your queen. [%cal Rg7f6] } 22... gxf6 { A queen for a knight, and his queen still stands. He is winning. [%csl Rf6] }) (22. Rc1 { A rook move, and his queen simply takes your bishop first. } 22... Qxb6 { She ate your bishop, and you are now two pieces down for a pawn. You are lost. [%csl Rb6] }) 22... Rxd8 { His rook had to take, and it has left b8. } 23. Qxb7 { A queen for a rook. The check pulled his rook off b8, and the queen it guarded fell. That is deflection. } (23. Bxd8 { The bishop takes the rook, but his queen takes yours on f3. } 23... Qxf3 24. gxf3 Kxd8 25. Rd1+ Kc7 { The queens are off, and he has two extra pieces for a pawn. You are lost. [%csl Rc7] }) (23. Qxf6 { A knight is far smaller than the queen standing on b7. } 23... gxf6 24. Bxd8 Kxd8 25. Rd1+ Kc7 { Your rook, queen and bishop went for his rook and knight: you are down a queen and a bishop for a pawn, and you are lost. [%csl Rc7] }) (23. Qc6+ { A check, but his queen just takes yours. [%cal Rb7c6] } 23... Qxc6 { Your queen is gone for nothing. You are down a queen, a rook and a knight, and you are lost. [%csl Rc6] }) (23. Rd1 { Another rook to d1, but his rook takes it with check. [%cal Rd8d1] } 23... Rxd1+ { Your last rook is gone, and his queen is still on the board. You are lost. [%csl Rd1] }) 1-0


