{ You are White. Next trick: the decoy. Force a piece onto a square you chose, where something is waiting for it. You are a piece down here. }
17... Re8 { His rook challenges yours on the e column, and that is a mistake: his rook belonged on c8. Take it with check, and his king has to land on e8. [%cal Ye1e8] } 18. Rxe8+ { Check. His king takes back, and it lands on e8, one knight jump from c7. [%csl Re8] } (18. Red1 { Keeping your rook lets him keep his king on d8, far from your knight. } 18... Re6 19. Rd2 Rc8 20. Rad1 Rd6 { His pieces are all guarded, and you are still a piece down. He is winning. [%csl Gd8] }) (18. c4 { A quiet pawn move lets him trade rooks on his own terms. } 18... Rxe1+ 19. Rxe1 Bd6 20. b4 b6 { His king stayed on d8, the fork never happened, and you are still a piece down. [%csl Gd8] }) 18... Kxe8 { His king has landed on e8. Your knight can check it from c7, and take the bishop there as well. [%cal Yd5c7] } 19. Nxc7+ { Check, and the bishop is gone. From c7 your knight also hits his rook on a8. Watch it collect. [%csl Ra8][%tail] } (19. Re1+ { A second check lets his king slip back to d8, where it guards c7. [%csl Rc7] } 19... Kd8 20. Re7 Rc8 21. Rxf7 Be5 { You won the f7 pawn, but you are still a piece down for two pawns. He is winning. [%csl Re5] }) (19. Rd1 { A calm rook move gives his bishop time to leave c7. } 19... Bd8 20. c3 Nb6 21. Nxb6 axb6 { The knights are traded, and you are still a piece down. [%csl Rd8] }) 19... Kd8 20. Nxa8 { The knight takes the rook on a8. The check dragged his king onto e8, where the fork was waiting: that is the decoy. You started a piece down, and now you are a rook up. } 1-0


