{ You are Black, with a queen and a knight against his queen and rook, and your king is stuck on the edge. The decoy: find where to drag his king. }
30. Qf2 { He offers the queens off: a mistake. His queen belonged on d6, hitting your loose knight on b4. Now a trade drags his king where it should never go. } 30... Qxf2+ { You take with check, and his king has to take back, or he is simply a queen down. [%csl Rf2] } (30... Qe7 { Your queen steps back, and his rook comes to the open column. } 31. Rf1 { His rook owns the f column and your king is still boxed in. He is better. [%csl Rf1] }) (30... Qd7 { Another retreat, and his queen gets to f8 behind your lines. } 31. Qf8 { His queen is in your camp, and he is much better. [%csl Rf8] }) 31. Kxf2 { His king takes back on f2. Look at where it stands, and at his rook on c1. [%csl Rf2,Rc1] } 31... Nd3+ { Check, and the knight attacks his rook on c1 as well. He must move the king, so the rook is lost. [%cal Gd3f2,Gd3c1] } (31... Kb7 { A king move, and his rook steps out of the knight's reach. } 32. Ra1 { His rook is safe and his king is active. He is better. [%csl Ra1] }) (31... h6 { A pawn move, and he tidies up his pieces. } 32. Ke2 Kb5 33. Rc7 { His rook has reached the seventh row, and he is winning. [%csl Rc7] }) (31... b5 { A pawn push, and his king simply steps away from the fork. } 32. Ke2 { The fork is gone, and he is much better. [%csl Re2] }) 32. Kg3 { His king steps away, but he could not save both: the rook he left behind is still in your knight's reach. } 32... Nxc1 { The rook is yours. The queen trade dragged his king to f2, exactly where your knight could fork it: that is the decoy. A rook for nothing. [%csl Gc1] } (32... b5 { A pawn push with his rook still on the board, and the rook attacks your pawns. } 33. Rc7 { His rook is active and safe, and he is much better. [%csl Rc7] }) (32... Kb7 { You walk the king instead of taking, and his rook runs to the open column, checking you a move later. } 33. Rf1 Nb4 34. Rf7+ { His rook checks you along the seventh row, and he is winning. [%csl Rf7] }) (32... Nb2 { The knight steps away from the rook it should take. } 33. Rc7 { His rook is safe and attacks your pawns. He is winning. [%csl Rc7] }) 0-1


