{ You are Black, a pawn down, with your knight planted in the middle. His bishop is about to join the fight, and the order of your moves decides everything. }
21. Bf4 { His bishop attacks your queen, and that is the mistake: the bishop belonged on h4. His queen is now the only guard of that bishop. } 21... Qxf4 { You take the bishop with the queen. If his queen takes back, she lands on f4, and a knight check is waiting for her there. [%csl Rf4] } (21... Ne2+ { The fork first, but his queen takes the knight, and then the bishop is all you get. } 22. Qxe2 Qxf4 23. Re1 { A knight for a bishop, and his rook is active. He is slightly better. [%csl Re1] }) (21... Bxc3 { You grab a pawn with the bishop, but his bishop grabs one back on b7 and hits your rook. } 22. Bxb7 Bxd2 23. Bxd6 { Both queens went, and his bishops are the better pair. He is better. [%csl Rd6] }) (21... Qe7 { Your queen steps back, and his rook takes the open column. } 22. Re1 { He is active everywhere, and he is better. [%csl Re1] }) 22. Qxf4 { He takes back with the queen; a retreat would have lost the bishop just the same. Now his queen stands on f4 with no guard at all. [%csl Rf4] } 22... Ne2+ { Check, and the knight attacks his queen on f4 as well. He must move his king, and his queen is lost. [%cal Ge2g1,Ge2f4] } (22... Bxc3 { You grab a pawn, and his queen escapes with threats of her own. } 23. Qe4 { His queen is safe, hitting your knight and the b7 pawn, and your bishop on c3 hangs too. He is winning. [%csl Re4] }) (22... Ne6 { Your knight attacks the queen, and she takes a pawn with tempo. } 23. Qxf6 { She is safe, and you are down a queen for a piece. He is winning. [%csl Rf6] }) (22... Nc6 { The knight retreats, and his queen takes a pawn. } 23. Qxf6 { She is safe and hunting your king. He is winning. [%csl Rf6] }) 23. Kf1 { His king steps aside, but he could not save both: his queen is still in your knight's reach. } 23... Nxf4 { His queen is yours. Your queen took the bishop so his queen would land on f4, where the knight forked her: that is the decoy. A piece up for a pawn. [%csl Gf4] } (23... Re8 { Your rook comes to the open column, and his queen escapes with a pawn. } 24. Qxf6 { She is safe, and he is winning. [%csl Rf6] }) (23... Bxc3 { A pawn, and his king takes your knight. } 24. Kxe2 { A knight for a pawn, and he is winning. [%csl Re2] }) (23... Kg7 { A king move, and his king takes the knight. } 24. Kxe2 { Your knight is gone for nothing. He is winning. [%csl Re2] }) 0-1


