{ You are White. Pieces everywhere in the middle, and his queen on b4 is eyeing your pawns. }
13... Qxb2 { His queen takes the pawn on b2, and that is the mistake: his rooks had to come to the middle. She is a long way from the knight she guards on e5. [%csl Rb2,Re5] } 14. Bd4 { Your bishop lands on d4, right on that diagonal, and attacks his queen. From b2 she can no longer see the knight. [%cal Rd4b2] } (14. Rxf6 { Taking the knight on f6 costs your rook: his pawn takes back. } 14... gxf6 15. Nf4 Qa3 16. Bd4 Qe7 { A rook for a knight, and his queen got back to e7 to guard e5. The clean win is gone. [%cal Re7e5] }) (14. a3 { A pawn move that guards nothing, and his bishop joins in. } 14... Ba6 15. Bd4 Bxe2 16. Bxb2 Bxd1 17. Bxe5 Bxc2 { A flurry of captures, both queens gone, and the position is about level. [%csl Gc2] }) (14. Rb1 { Your rook attacks his queen, but she simply steps to a3. Nothing hits the knight yet, and next move she comes home to guard it. } 14... Qa3 15. Rb3 Qe7 { She came home to e7, his knight is guarded, and the position is level. [%csl Ge7] }) (14. Bxb6 { You grab a pawn, and his pawn takes your bishop. [%cal Ra7b6] } 14... axb6 { A bishop for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rb6] }) 14... Qa3 { His queen runs to a3. What was she guarding before she left the diagonal? } 15. Bxe5 { A knight for the pawn he grabbed. Your bishop on d4 cut the line from his queen to e5: that is interference. } (15. Rxf6 { The knight on f6 was guarded, and his pawn takes back. } 15... gxf6 16. Nf4 c5 17. dxc6 dxc6 { A rook for a knight, the free knight on e5 was left alone, and it is about level. [%csl Ge5] }) (15. Rf5 { Another attacker on f6, but his d pawn is about to cover e5. [%csl Re5] } 15... d6 16. Rxf6 gxf6 17. Nf4 f5 { The d6 pawn guards e5 now, and the position is level. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (15. Rb1 { Your rook on b1 attacks nothing of value, and his queen comes home to e7 to guard the knight. } 15... Qe7 16. Ba1 d6 { His knight is guarded by the pawn now, and he is slightly better. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (15. Bxb6 { A pawn, while the knight was there for free, and his pawn takes your bishop. [%cal Ra7b6] } 15... axb6 { His pawn ate your bishop. He is winning. [%csl Rb6] }) 1-0


