{ You are White. New trick: the in between move. When he takes something, do not take back at once. Look first for a stronger move, like a check he has to answer. }
14... Qxd4 { His queen takes your knight on d4, and that is the mistake: his knight should have taken instead. Now his queen attacks yours. Before you take back, look at his knight on c6: it is what would recapture. [%cal Yg2c6] } 15. Bxc6+ { Check! Your bishop takes the knight that guarded d4. He has to answer the check first, and then nothing guards his queen. [%cal Gc6e8,Ge3d4] } (15. Qxd4 { Taking back at once trades the queens, and his knight lands in the middle. } 15... Nxd4 { The queens are gone, his knight sits on d4, and he is winning. [%csl Rd4] }) (15. Qf3 { Your queen runs, and his queen stays in the middle guarded by the knight. } 15... Rd8 { His pieces are active and he is a knight up. He is winning. [%csl Rd4] }) (15. Nb5 { Your knight attacks his queen, and she simply takes yours. [%cal Rd4e3] } 15... Qxe3 16. fxe3 { The queens are off, and you are a knight down. He is winning. [%csl Re3] }) 15... bxc6 { His pawn takes back. His queen is still on d4, and the knight that guarded her is gone. Now take her. [%cal Ye3d4] } 16. Qxd4 { A queen for your bishop. The check came in between: it removed the recapturer, and then you took back. That is the in between move. [%csl Gd4] } (16. Qe4 { You offer a queen trade instead of taking, and he declines. } 16... Qb6 { His queen is safe, he is a piece up, and he is winning. [%csl Rb6] }) (16. Qf3 { Your queen leaves the column, and his queen is safe. } 16... Rc8 { He is a piece up and winning. [%csl Rd4] }) (16. Rac1 { A rook move, and his queen takes yours. [%cal Rd4e3] } 16... Qxe3 17. fxe3 { The queens came off anyway, and you are a piece down. He is winning. [%csl Re3] }) 1-0


