{ You are White. His bishop on a7 and both his rooks point at your king. The in between move: find it. }
33... Bxf2 { His bishop takes your pawn on f2, and that is the mistake: his rook had to take yours first. Before you take back, look around. } 34. Rxh3 { You take the rook first: he has to take back, and his bishop is still hanging after that. } (34. Kxf2 { Taking the bishop at once lets his rooks come off in his favour. } 34... Rxh1 35. Rxh1 Rxh1 36. Ke3 f5 { You are the exchange and a pawn down, and he is winning. [%csl Rh1] }) (34. Bd2 { Saving your bishop lets the rooks trade on h1. } 34... Rxh1 35. Rxh1 Rxh1 36. Kxh1 Bxg3 { All the rooks are gone, and he is two pawns up. He is winning. [%csl Rg3] }) (34. Rhf1 { Your rook attacks the bishop, and his rook mates you on the spot. [%cal Rh3h2] } 34... Rh2# { Mate! His rook drops in beside your king, guarded by his other rook behind it, and the bishop takes g1 away. [%csl Rg2] }) (34. Bxf6 { A pawn, and his rook takes yours in the corner. [%cal Rh3h1] } 34... Rxh1 35. Rxh1 Rxh1 { A clean rook down, and your king must not take it back: then his d pawn runs to d1 and queens. He is winning. [%csl Rh1] }) (34. Rhd1 { Your rook runs from the corner, and his rook checks you into a mating net. [%cal Rh3h2] } 34... Rh2+ 35. Kf1 Bb6 { His bishop steps back out of reach, and he mates in three. [%csl Rf1] }) 34... gxh3+ { He takes back with the g pawn, with check. } 35. Kxf2 { And now the bishop. Rook first, bishop second: that is the in between move. A bishop up for a pawn. } (35. Kh2 { Your king dodges the check, and his bishop on f2 stays alive. } 35... Be3 36. Rd1 d2 37. Bxf6 Re8 { His bishop escaped and his pawn on d2 is close to queening. It is about level. [%csl Rd2] }) (35. Kf3 { Your king steps away, and his h pawn runs through. } 35... h2 36. Kxf2 h1=Q 37. Rxh1 Rxh1 { His pawn queened and took your rook with it. He is winning. [%csl Rh1] }) (35. Kh1 { Into the corner, and his bishop lives on. It takes your g pawn next. } 35... Bxg3 { His bishop ate a second pawn, his passed pawn on d3 is strong, and he is winning. [%csl Rg3] }) (35. Kf1 { Your king steps back and his bishop escapes with a pawn. } 35... Bxg3 { His bishop is safe and he is two pawns up with a passed pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rg3] }) 1-0


