{ You are Black, and his queen is deep in your camp. Your own back row has no air. }
22. Qxd5 { His queen takes your knight, and that is the mistake: she should have taken the pawn on a7 instead. He is sure you cannot take back. [%csl Rd5] } 22... Qxe1+ { Your queen takes his rook with check. He must take back with the bishop, and then nothing guards his queen. [%cal Ge1g1] } (22... Rxd5 { You take the queen at once, and his rook mates you on the back row. [%cal Re1e8] } 23. Re8# { Mate. Your back row had no air, and his rook used it. [%csl Rg8] }) (22... Qxc2 { A pawn, and his queen takes your rook with mate. [%cal Rd5d8] } 23. Qxd8# { Mate. Your back row had no air, and that rook was its only guard. [%csl Rg8] }) (22... Qf6 { Your queen comes back to guard the back row, but you stay a knight down. } 23. Qc5 { He is a piece up, and he is winning. [%csl Rc5] }) (22... h6 { You give your king air, and his queen takes your rook with check. } 23. Qxd8+ Kh7 24. Qd3+ { Check again, and he has a rook and a bishop more than you. You are lost. [%csl Rd3] }) 23. Bxe1 { His bishop takes your queen back. His queen on d5 is still there, and nothing guards her now. } 23... Rxd5 { His queen is yours. Taking at once walked into mate; the rook first, with check: that is the in between move. Rook against bishop, a pawn up. [%csl Gd5] } (23... Re8 { Your rook chases the bishop instead of taking the queen. } 24. Bd2 { His queen is safe: queen and bishop against your rook. He is winning. [%csl Rd5] }) (23... Rc8 { Your rook steps aside, and his queen is safe. } 24. Bc3 { Queen and bishop against your lone rook. He is winning. [%csl Rd5] }) (23... h5 { A pawn move, and his queen takes your rook with check. } 24. Qxd8+ { Your rook is gone with check. He has queen and bishop, you have only pawns. You are lost. [%csl Rd8] }) 0-1


