15... Nxd3 { His knight takes your pawn on d3, and that is the mistake: he had to take your bishop first. Before you take back, look at the whole board. } 16. Bxe6+ { Check first: the bishop takes on e6. He has to answer the check, and his knight on d3 can wait. } (16. cxd3 { Taking back at once lets his bishop take yours on b3. } 16... Bxb3 17. Kg1 Re8 18. Qg4+ Be6 { Your bishop went for his knight, pieces are level, and you are two pawns down. He is much better. [%csl Re6] }) (16. Kg1 { Your king tucks away, and his knight grabs b2 as well. } 16... Nxb2 17. Bd4 Nh6 { He is three pawns up and his knights are safe. He is much better. [%csl Rb2] }) (16. Qxd8+ { You take the rook, and his king takes your queen. [%cal Rc8d8] } 16... Kxd8 { A queen for a rook, and his knight still sits on d3. He is winning. [%csl Rd8] }) (16. Bxa7 { A pawn on the edge, while his knight takes your bishop on b3. [%cal Rd3b3] } 16... Bxb3 { A bishop for a pawn: if your pawn takes on b3, his queen takes your bishop on a7. He is winning. [%csl Rb3] }) (16. Bd4 { Your bishop centralises, but his knight takes your other bishop first. [%cal Rd3b3] } 16... Bxb3 17. cxb3 Qf5+ { His queen checks, his knight is safe, and he is two pawns up. He is better. [%csl Rd3] }) 16... fxe6 { His pawn takes back. His other answers to the check lose even more. } 17. cxd3 { Now the knight too. Bishops traded evenly, then you took his knight: that is the in between move. A piece up for two pawns. } (17. Qe4 { This aims at the knight, and it jumps away to e5. } 17... Ne5 18. b4 Qa6+ 19. b5 Qa5 { His knight is safe on e5, he is two pawns up, and he is better. [%csl Re5] }) (17. b4 { A pawn move gives his knight time to escape, and his queen checks first. } 17... Qf5+ 18. Kg1 Ne5 { His knight got away to e5 and his queen is active. You are two pawns down, and he is much better. [%csl Re5] }) (17. Qxd8+ { Rook for queen is a bad trade: his king takes her. [%cal Rc8d8] } 17... Kxd8 { His king took your queen, and a rook is all you have for her. He is winning. [%csl Rd8] }) (17. Bxa7 { A pawn, and his rook checks you on the open column. [%cal Rd8f8] } 17... Rf8+ 18. Kg1 Qxa7+ { His queen took the bishop back with check, and his knight is still on d3. He is winning. [%csl Ra7] }) 1-0


