{ You are Black. Last trick: the trapped piece. Attack a piece that has no safe square left, and it is yours. }
13. Qxb7 { His queen takes the pawn on b7, and that is the mistake: she should have stayed home. Send your knight to a5 to attack her. [%cal Yc6a5] } 13... Na5 { The knight attacks his queen. Every square she can reach is covered, so she has no safe square left. [%csl Rb7] } (13... Ra7 { The rook chases her, but she slips back to b3 with your pawn. } 14. Qb3 O-O 15. Rg1 Qe7 { She escaped with the b7 pawn, and the position is about level. [%csl Gb7] }) (13... Bd7 { Guarding the knight lets her walk home. } 14. Qb3 Be6 15. Qa4 Bd7 { She is safe on a4 with your pawn, and the position is level. [%csl Ga4] }) (13... Ne7 { A quiet knight move, and she gets time to leave. } 14. Ne5 O-O 15. f4 Rb8 { She keeps the b7 pawn and can still run, and the position is about level. [%csl Gb7] }) 14. Qxa6 { She grabs one more pawn on a6 on her way down. } 14... Rxa6 { Your rook takes the queen. Now watch. [%tail] } (14... O-O { Castling lets her walk out to e2. } 15. Qe2 Nc4 16. Ne5 Nxe5 17. dxe5 { She escaped. He is two pawns up for now, and even after your bishop wins one back it is only about level. [%csl Ge2] }) (14... Nc4 { This chases her again, and she escapes with check on b5. } 15. Qb5+ c6 16. Qb7 Nxd2 { Your knight took his bishop, but she is still loose. About level, not a queen up. [%csl Gb7] }) 15. Bxa6 Qa8 { Your queen goes to a8 and hits his bishop on a6. A queen for a rook and two pawns: she was trapped, so she was yours. [%cal Ga8a6] } 0-1


