{ You are White, and his queen is about to raid your pawns. The trapped piece: a queen that goes hunting can run out of squares. }
16... Qxb2 { His queen takes the pawn on b2, and that is the mistake: his rook belonged on c8. Now she stands deep in your camp, attacking your rook. [%csl Rb2] } 17. Bc3 { Your bishop attacks the queen and cuts off her way back. Every square she can reach is covered, so she has to take the bishop. [%cal Gc3b2] } (17. Qc3 { You offer a queen trade, and he takes it, a pawn up. } 17... Qxc3 18. Bxc3 { The queens are off and he is two pawns up. He is winning. [%csl Rc3] }) (17. Rc1 { Your rook steps away, and his rooks come to the open columns. } 17... Rfc8 { His queen is safe and his rooks are coming. He is winning. [%csl Rc8] }) (17. Ra2 { You attack her with the rook, and she checks. } 17... Qb1+ { Your king is exposed and she is loose in your camp. He is winning. [%csl Rb1] }) (17. Qxh7+ { A pawn with check, and his king takes your queen. [%cal Rg8h7] } 17... Kxh7 { A queen for a pawn. You are lost. [%csl Rh7] }) 17... Qxc3 { His queen takes the bishop, the only move she had left. } 18. Qxc3 { Your queen takes her back. She had no safe square: that is the trapped piece. A queen for a bishop and two pawns. [%csl Gc3] } (18. Ra2 { A rook move, and his queen checks on d4 and lives. } 18... Qd4+ { She is still on the board, and he is winning. [%csl Rd4] }) (18. Qe3 { Your queen attacks his, and she takes your rook with check. [%cal Rc3a1] } 18... Qxa1+ { A rook gone, and his queen still alive. You are lost. [%csl Ra1] }) (18. Rc1 { The rook comes to c1, but your pawn on c2 blocks it from the queen, and she checks first. } 18... Qd4+ { Your king is in the open and she is loose. You are lost. [%csl Rd4] }) 1-0


