{ You are White, in a rook ending where his rook is deep in your camp. }
49... Rh3 { His rook steps to h3 to attack your pawn, and that is the mistake: he should have traded rooks. Now his rook has very few squares. [%csl Rh3] } 50. Kg2 { Your king attacks the rook. It cannot go back along the column, your pawn covers h4, and on g3 it would be taken too. [%cal Gg2h3,Gg3h4] } (50. Rf3 { Your rook guards the pawn but does not touch his rook, and it simply steps out of the net. } 50... Rh2 { His rook is safe, and the position is level. [%csl Gh2] }) (50. e5 { A pawn push, and his rook takes a pawn. } 50... Rxg3 { He is two pawns up, and he is much better. [%csl Rg3] }) (50. Rxf6 { You grab a pawn, and his king takes your rook. [%cal Rg7f6] } 50... Kxf6 { Your rook went for a single pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rf6] }) 50... Rh1 { His rook runs to the corner: it was lost wherever it went. } 51. Kxh1 { A whole rook. It had no safe square: that is the trapped piece. [%csl Gh1] } (51. e5 { You push a pawn instead of taking, and his rook slips out. } 51... Re1 { His rook escaped, and the position is level. [%csl Ge1] }) (51. Rc2 { Your rook goes sideways, and his rook escapes. } 51... Re1 { The rook got out, and the position is level. [%csl Ge1] }) 1-0


